<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#muhtasib">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhtasib</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as amil al-suq, sahib al-suq, muhtesip</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A muhtasib was the holder of the office of hisbah in classical Islamic administrations: a supervisor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> and trade and inspector of public places and behaviour in towns, appointed by the sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> or another authority to see that public business followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>. The office is commonly held to succeed the Byzantine agoranomos, a market overseer also known in Parthian and Sassanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> the muhtasib regulated weights, money, prices, public morals and cleanliness, and had cheating merchants whipped and paraded; manuals such as al-Shayzari's guided the work. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> abolished the office in Cairo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 1837.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhtasib.jpg" alt="Muhtasib" width="330" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claes Rålamb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ralamb-93.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Islamic tradition holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> himself appointed overseers of the markets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, among them Saʿid b. Saʿid b. al-As over the market of Medina after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-mecca">conquest of Mecca</a> in 629, and that the caliph Umar had two men working over that market. During the Umayyad period market inspectors are reported at Mecca and Wāsit, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the post was called Sāḥib al-Sūq. According to R. P. Buckley, the first muhtasibs are mentioned in the early years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and the new name may have been intended to indicate an Islamicizing of the post.</p><p>Willem Floor argues that the conquered societies already had such market inspectors, that the practical manuals came before those setting the office in a religious and legal frame, and that the jurists imposed that frame on an existing institution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ghazali">Al-Ghazali</a> (d. 1111) used the word muhtasib in a wider sense, for anyone who forbids wrong, which the historian Michael Cook sees as a source of confusion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The muhtasib stood roughly between a qadi and a court magistrate: he could not hear cases, only settle disputes and breaches of the law where the facts were admitted or guilt confessed. Besides prices, weights and food, he inspected public eating houses and kept a check on doctors, surgeons and apothecaries, and craftsmen and builders answered to him for the standards of their craft. After 950, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, the office was sold, in 961 for 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> a month, and Floor thinks its holders took bribes to recover the cost. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> wrote that the post was usually given to a noble, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> or an old Turk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Under the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, who ruled from 1658 to 1707, muhtasibs acted as censors of morals enforcing his orders, including the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and shrines. The office was renewed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1855 and in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1925. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> it was abolished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> around 1852 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1877, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> it survived as a city cleaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> until 1926. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> the möxtäsip enforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> until the 1920s. In post-Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> a muhtasib is a regional representative of a muftiate, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> the Mohtasib is an ombudsman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhtasib” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the chief qadis served deputy judges, preachers, prayer leaders and the teachers of the many madrasas that sultans and amirs built and endowed in Cairo, Damascus and Jerusalem, and until the 15th century a scholar or qadi usually held the post of <b>muhtasib</b>, the inspector of Cairo’s markets.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
